      "SummaryText": "Mobile\n\n(a) ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION AND EMERGENCY RESPONSE POLICIES\n\nSterling Specialty Chemicals is a leader among chemical manufacturers in its safety, health and environmental programs.  Our commitment to protect our employees and the communities that are home to our operations is an integral part of our way of doing business.  Sterling has established safety, health, emergency response and environmental policies and standards to meet all regulatory requirements.\n\n(b) THE STATIONARY SOURCE AND THE REGULATED SUBSTANCES HANDLED\n\nOur facility has 3 processes that involve a regulated substance.\n\nThe Pilot Plant makes water-soluble organic polymers and mining chemicals used for the development of new technologies and improving on existing formulations in the mining, water treating and paper markets.   The regulated substance involved with this operation is 29.4% ammonium hydroxide solution.\n\nThe polyacrylamide emulsion manufacturing process (EPAM) makes water-soluble organic polymers that are used to remove solids from a number of different wastewater applications.   The regulated substance involved with this operation is 29.4% ammonium hydroxide solution\n\nThe Bio-Acrylamide manufacturing process (Bio-AMD) makes Bio-AMD from acrylonitrile and biological enzymes for use as a raw material in the Pilot Plant and EPAM processes along with shipment to other plants as a raw material in their processes. \n\n(c) THE GENERAL ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION PROGRAM AND CHEMICAL-SPECIFIC PREVENTION STEPS\n\nSterling's general accidental release prevention program includes:\n\n- EPA 40 CFR 68 - Risk Management Plans\n- OSHA 29 CFR 1910.119 - Process Safety Management of Highly Hazardous Chemicals \n- OSHA 29 CFR 1910.120 - Hazardous Waste Operations & Emergency Response\n- Other EPA, OSHA, DOT and State regulations\n- Generally recognized & accepted engineering codes and standards, and\n- Sterling Safety, Health, Environmental and Engineering standards\n\nThe following engineering and adm"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124092,
      "SummaryText": "inistrative controls help prevent a release of ammonium hydroxide and acrylonitrile:\n- Process Hazard Analysis Reviews (PHA)\n- Pre-Start up Safety Reviews (PSSR)\n- Storage tank high level alarm/interlock\n- Storage tank high level switch and associated interlock\n- Loading/unloading vapor return system\n- Written process instructions for using aqua ammonia and acrylonitrile\n- Diking around the storage tanks\n- Storage tank equipped with conservation vent\n- Acrylonitrile monitors around the storage tanks\n- Fire detection and suppression (Foam) in the acrylonitrile storage tank area\n- Water mist cooling on the acrylonitrile storage tanks\n- ROX inerting of the acrylonitrile tank head space \n- Preventative maintenance procedures\n- Constant personnel attendance during transfers\n\n(d) 5-YEAR ACCIDENT HISTORY\n\nOn October 23, 2024 an employee preparing to unload an ammonium hydroxide truck removed the camlock cap from the unloading hose. The hose contained a quantity of 29% ammonium hydroxide that had not been flushed after the previous unload. the ammonium hydroxide in the hose had heated and expanded creating pressure in the hose. When he took the cap off the vapor and liquid vented and sprayed on the employee. The employee was fully suited in a full- face respirator and acid suit. He went to the safety shower where he took off his respirator and inhaled the vapors coming off of his acid suit. Employee was transported to the local ER for evaluation and released. Root cause of the incident was failure to follow appropriate checklists and safety procedures during previous unload.\n\n(e) EMERGENCY RESPONSE PROGRAM\n\nThe Sterling Mobile Plant maintains emergency procedures that are designed to protect, to the greatest extent possible, the employees of the plant and the surrounding community.  The procedures are designed to be implemented in case of fire, explosion, chemical release which may arise as a result of our own operations, adjacent operations or the forces of nature.  These"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000124092,
      "SummaryText": "procedures are set up to be implemented in two primary areas; 1.) in plant and 2.) in conjunction with the community.\n\nThe in plant portion of the emergency response plan provides for employee notification of an emergency through an alarm system and in plant response with operations personnel to take immediate remedial actions.  The Mobile Fire and Rescue Hazmat Team and fully trained and equipped contract services are readily available.  The in plant emergency response protocols also provide for communications, notifications, procedures for emergency shut down, and mitigation.  The in plant system is tested on a frequency ranging from weekly, for the alarm and communications system, to bi-annually for in-plant musters and drills.\n\nIn addition to the in plant portion of the emergency response plan, the Mobile Plant also maintains an active community based emergency response program.  This is done through participation in the Mobile County LEPC as well as with the Mobile County Emergency Management Agency which coordinates mutual aid for the entire Mobile area chemical manufacturing community.  Participation is achieved through planning meetings, public forums, annual emergency drills.  The Sterling Plant also interfaces frequently with community based emergency response groups such as the fire department and local hazardous materials response team.  Plant tours and readiness evaluations for these organizations are conducted on an annual basis.\n\n(f) PLANNED CHANGES TO IMPROVE SAFETY\n\nSterling Specialty Chemicals, LLC, is constantly striving to upgrade its processes, control technology, employee training, inspection, repair and maintenance of process equipment in order to improve safety.\n\nSpecific improvements currently planned that will directly improve safety in the plant include:\n\n- Implementation of a Behavioral Based Safety Program. \n- Set KPIs for participation and hazard awareness response time. \n- Improve quality of observations by including hazard recognition"
